e have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nM⇒¬N≡¬M∨¬N\n$$\n\nUsing De Morgan's law, we reach\n\n$$\n¬M∨¬N≡¬(M∧N).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬(M∧N).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Win this game, or you won't make it to the playoffs.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "283e6f42-c6ed-5da0-9085-3d8a16902757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411059 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Win this game.\n\n- $B:$ You won’t make it to the playoffs.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't win this game.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If you don't wake up early, then you won't have time for breakfast.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "442f3c28-f104-5684-ab21-f7ea922bad92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411060 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't wake up early.\n\n- $B:$ You won't have time for breakfast.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You wake up early.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You wake up early, or you won't have time for breakfast.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Wake up early, or you won't have time for breakfast." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬P⇒Q).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cc8fa9c-919a-5715-b893-d15125898b55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411061 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(¬(¬P)∨Q).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬P)∨Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not study for the test, or you will get a good grade.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64778f38-4137-5738-8e84-69949fb75847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411062 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not study for the test.\n\n- $B:$ You will get a good grade.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study for the test.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you study for the test, then you will get a good grade.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you study for the test, you will get a good grade.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If you follow the recipe, the dish will be delicious.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "879f4dd5-148a-578f-a2fe-b0846abdf946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You follow the recipe.\n\n- $B:$ The dish will be delicious.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not follow the recipe.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not wear a seatbelt, or you'll be safe.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94ae2108-4770-5706-9cb7-823f98b8c863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not wear a seatbelt.\n\n- $B:$ You'll be safe.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You wear a seatbelt.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, then you'll be safe.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, you'll be safe.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒B≡¬A∨B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∨¬B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∧¬B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7ec246a-1c3b-5830-ae08-ed99cbcc20da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411065 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X⇒Y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb0a268b-ca46-5b91-9359-0e418e71bab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(X⇒Y)≡¬(¬X∨Y)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨Y)≡¬(¬X)∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
